   {"on": "let the speaker speak truly and the judge decide justly", "text": "Socrates asks the jury to do its proper job. In Athenian courts defendants usually wept, brought their children and flattered the jury. Socrates refuses all that, and the jury will notice."},
   {"on": "telling of one Socrates, a wise man, who speculated about the heaven above, and searched into the earth beneath, and made the worse appear the better cause", "text": "Here are the 'old charges': the picture from The Clouds that you read in session 1. 'Making the worse appear the better cause' was what sophists were accused of: winning arguments for the wrong side."},
