   {"on": "for I know that I have no wisdom, small or great", "text": "This is the start of the famous paradox. Socrates does not doubt the god; he doubts his own understanding of the god's words. He treats the oracle as a riddle to be solved, which is how Greeks treated oracles."},
   {"on": "I neither know nor think that I know", "text": "Here is the original of 'I know that I know nothing'. Socrates never says exactly that in Plato. What he says is narrower and more precise: I do not think I know what I do not know. The difference matters. He is not claiming to know nothing at all; he knows, for instance, that doing wrong is evil. He claims to know the limits of his knowledge."},
   {"on": "I found that the men most in repute were all but the most foolish; and that others less esteemed were really wiser and better", "text": "A dangerous thing to say to a jury of ordinary citizens about their leaders, and a democratic thing too: reputation is not wisdom."},
   {"on": "not by wisdom do poets write poetry, but by a sort of genius and inspiration", "text": "The poets, Homer's heirs, were the teachers of Greece. Socrates finds that they cannot explain what they have written. Inspiration is not understanding."},
